Yet another approach — the "algebraic torus model": represent torus functions as functions `f : (Fin n → ℤ) →₀ ℂ` (finitely supported), i.e., the space of finite Fourier series, and define the Laplacian as multiplication by -4π²|k|² on the k-th mode. Then the "eigenfunction" statement is: `Δ(e_k) = -4π²|k|² e_k` where e_k are basis elements (delta functions on ℤⁿ). Hmm, that would trivially define e_k as eigenfunctions by definition of the Laplacian, which is circular/not "explicit computation". The task explicitly says "eigenvalues 4π²|k|² (explicit computation)".

Best faithful approach: define the torus as the quotient, define Fourier mode e_k as above, define the Laplacian as `Δf = ∑ᵢ ∂ᵢ²f` where ∂ᵢ is the i-th coordinate derivative of the smooth function on the torus. Computing Δe_k explicitly: ∂ᵢ e_k = 2πi k_i e_k, ∂ᵢ² e_k = (2πi k_i)² e_k = -4π² k_i² e_k, so Δ e_k = -4π² |k|² e_k. So the eigenfunction equation is Δ e_k = -4π²|k|² e_k — negative eigenvalues. The task says "eigenfunctions of the Laplacian with eigenvalues 4π²|k|²", consistent with the sign convention Δ = -∑∂ᵢ² (geometer's Laplacian) or they just state the magnitude. I'll define the Laplacian with the geometer's sign convention: Δ = -∑ᵢ ∂ᵢ². Then Δ e_k = 4π²|k|² e_k. That matches "eigenvalues 4π²|k|²".

To do this in Lean, I need:
